  /* Countries in letterspaced mono caps against everything else's mixed-case
     sans. Telling ranks of name apart by their setting rather than by a legend
     is the oldest convention on any map.

     It declares no colour and no weight, which is the whole of its current
     rank: it is a city label in a different setting. It ran at `fg-body` for
     several rounds and at `fg` AND weight 500 for one, and that round is the
     mistake to remember — a country name is context, it exists so the earth is
     not blank, and there are three times as many of them on screen as there are
     cities, spread in wide caps across every landmass. "Two ranks must stay a
     step apart" is true, and it does not follow that the step should be taken by
     raising the BACKGROUND rank. Here it is not taken at all. */
  .mapglobe__frame :global(.mapglobe__place--country) {
    @apply font-mono text-[0.62rem] tracking-[0.1em] uppercase;
  }

  /* The only atlas rank that steps off the base — brightest in dark, darkest
     in light.

     It has been three things: `fg-meta` as shipped, then `fg`, then the
     `color-mix` step between `fg` and `fg-body`. Both of the last two were
     reaching for separation from the trip cards, and `fg-body` gets it more
     cheaply than either — the card is 12px at weight 600 inside a bordered box
     in `fg`, and this is 10.2px at 400 with nothing around it, a full token
     below. The mix bought a rung that no longer had anything to sit between
     once the country came down to `fg-meta`.

     Measured on land: 4.74–6.25:1 across all nine themes, comfortably clear of
     AA at this size, and 7.83–11.07 against the halo.

     The ▲ and the height inherit it and declare no colour of their own, so a
     peak label reads as one object rather than a name with two accessories. */
  .mapglobe__frame :global(.mapglobe__place--peak) {
    @apply text-fg-body;
  }

  /* The ▲ that stands in for an icon layer — see `placeElement` in engine.ts.
